   "source": [
    "# Time Series Methods: Trend-Based Price Prediction\n",
    "\n",
    "## Learning Goals\n",
    "\n",
    "After completing this notebook, you will be able to:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Understand time series components (trend, seasonality, noise)\n",
    "- Implement and interpret simple time series models (trend, random walk)\n",
    "- Build and evaluate autoregressive models (AR, ARIMA, SARIMA)\n",
    "- Compare time series vs. cross-sectional approaches\n",
    "- Choose appropriate models based on data characteristics\n",
    "\n",
    "## Keywords\n",
    "\n",
    "time series, ARIMA, autoregressive, stationarity, forecasting, seasonality\n",
    "\n",
    "## Prerequisite Knowledge\n",
    "\n",
    "02_exploratory-analysis.ipynb (understanding of time trends)\n",
    "\n",
    "## Target User\n",
    "\n",
    "Analysts interested in forecasting approaches and time-dependent patterns"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Table of Contents\n",
    "\n",
    "1. [Part 1: Time Series Fundamentals](#part-1)\n",
    "2. [Part 2: Simple Time Series Models](#part-2)\n",
    "3. [Part 3: Autoregressive Models (AR, ARIMA, SARIMA)](#part-3)\n",
    "4. [Part 4: Time Series vs. Cross-Sectional Approaches](#part-4)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execution_count": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "## Part 1: Time Series Fundamentals {#part-1}\n",
    "\n",
    "### What Makes Time Series Different?\n",
    "\n",
    "In Notebook 3, we predicted price using a property's features (size, condition, economic indicators). That's **cross-sectional**: we look at many properties at one point in time.\n",
    "\n",
    "Now we ask: can we predict prices using only their **history**? That's **time series**: using past price values to forecast future prices.\n",
    "\n",
    "### When Time Series Works\n",
    "\n",
    "Time series forecasting works well when:\n",
    "- **Trends are strong**: Prices consistently rising or falling over time\n",
    "- **Patterns repeat**: Seasonal effects (e.g., spring sales higher than winter)\n",
    "- **Recent past predicts near future**: Current momentum continues\n",
    "\n",
    "Time series works poorly when:\n",
    "- **Many structural breaks**: Major economic shocks (2008 crisis)\n",
    "- **Random walk behavior**: Yesterday's price is best guess of today\n",
    "- **Long-term forecasts**: Trend changes often\n",
    "\n",
    "### Time Series Components"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execution_count": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "# A time series can be decomposed:\n",
    "# Price(t) = Trend + Seasonality + Noise\n",
    "\n",
    "# Trend: Long-term direction (rising/falling)\n",
    "# Seasonality: Regular patterns (e.g., Q2 always higher than Q1)\n",
    "# Noise: Random fluctuations\n",
    "\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "# Simulate components\n",
    "t = np.arange(0, 40)  # 40 quarters\n",
    "\n",
    "# Trend: gentle upward slope\n",
    "trend = 150000 + 2000 * t\n",
    "\n",
    "# Seasonality: Q1 and Q3 slightly higher\n",
    "seasonality = 5000 * np.cos(2 * np.pi * t / 4)  # 4-quarter cycle\n",
    "\n",
    "# Noise: random variation\n",
    "np.random.seed(42)\n",
    "noise = np.random.normal(0, 8000, len(t))\n",
    "\n",
    "# Combined\n",
    "price_series = trend + seasonality + noise\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plotting (would show in notebook)\n",
    "print(\"Time series components:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Trend:       Linear increase from {trend[0]:.0f} to {trend[-1]:.0f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Seasonality: Oscillates ±${5000:,.0f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Noise:       Random ±${8000:,.0f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"\\n  Combined series:\")\n",
    "print(f\"    Min: ${price_series.min():,.0f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"    Max: ${price_series.max():,.0f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"    Mean: ${price_series.mean():,.0f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execution_count": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "### Concept Check 1.1\n",
    "\n",
    "If housing prices show a strong upward trend but random walk behavior (each quarter is essentially the previous quarter plus noise), which is more predictive?\n",
    "\n",
    "A) The trend - it shows long-term direction\n",
    "B) The random walk - it captures short-term momentum\n",
    "C) Both equally - they capture different patterns\n",
    "D) Neither - random walk means prices are unpredictable\n",
    "\n",
    "<details>\n",
    "<summary>Answer</summary>\n",
    "B) The random walk captures short-term momentum better for 1-2 step-ahead forecasts. Even though the trend exists, if each quarter is (last quarter + noise), then \"yesterday = best guess of today\" beats any model using only trend. A random walk is actually quite good for short-term forecasting in markets. The trend emerges when averaging many random walk steps.\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execution_count": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "## Part 2: Simple Time Series Models {#part-2}\n",
    "\n",
    "### Linear Trend Model\n",
    "\n",
    "The simplest approach: fit a line through the price history and extrapolate forward.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Model**: Price(t) = α + β*t\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "- α = starting price level\n",
    "- β = price change per quarter\n",
    "- t = quarter number"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execution_count": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "# Linear trend on quarterly data\n",
    "# from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n",
    "\n",
    "# t = np.arange(len(quarterly_prices)).reshape(-1, 1)  # Quarter numbers 0, 1, 2, ...\n",
    "# trend_model = LinearRegression()\n",
    "# trend_model.fit(t, quarterly_prices)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Example results (hypothetical):\n",
    "# Coefficients: α = $145,000, β = $2,100 per quarter\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Linear Trend Results:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Starting price: $145,000\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Quarterly increase: $2,100 per quarter\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Implied annual increase: $2,100 × 4 = $8,400/year\")\n",
    "print(f\"  \\n  Train R²: 0.521\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Test R²:  0.412\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Test RMSE: $35,200\")\n",
    "print(\"\\nWhy it fails:\")\n",
    "print(\"- Assumes constant increase (unrealistic)\")\n",
    "print(\"- Ignores seasonality (Q2 might always be higher)\")\n",
    "print(\"- All predictions lie on a single line (oversimplified)\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execution_count": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "### Random Walk Model\n",
    "\n",
    "**Hypothesis**: Next quarter's price = last quarter's price + random noise\n",
    "\n",
    "**Model**: Price(t) = Price(t-1) + noise"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "# Random walk: \"best guess of next price is today's price\"\n",
    "\n",
    "# y_pred_rw = quarterly_prices[:-1]  # Shift prices down by 1\n",
    "# y_test_rw = quarterly_prices[1:]    # Test against actual next values\n",
    "\n",
    "# Example results (hypothetical):\n",
    "print(\"Random Walk Results:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Train R²: 0.732  (better than linear trend!)\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Test R²:  0.568  (much better than trend's 0.412)\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Test RMSE: $28,900 (better than trend's $35,200)\")\n",
    "print(\"\\nWhy it works:\")\n",
    "print(\"- Captures momentum (if price went up last quarter, likely up this quarter)\")\n",
    "print(\"- No assumptions about trend shape\")\n",
    "print(\"- Simple: just copies yesterday's value\")\n",
    "print(\"\\nWhy it's limited:\")\n",
    "print(\"- Can't predict new patterns (assumes past = future)\")\n",
    "print(\"- Poor for long-term forecasts\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execution_count": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "### Concept Check 2.1\n",
    "\n",
    "Linear Trend achieves Test R² = 0.412, Random Walk achieves 0.568. Which statement is true?\n",
    "\n",
    "A) Trend is simpler, so should be preferred\n",
    "B) Random Walk is clearly better (0.568 > 0.412)\n",
    "C) They capture different patterns; could combine them\n",
    "D) Neither works well enough for deployment\n",
    "\n",
    "<details>\n",
    "<summary>Answer</summary>\n",
    "C) They capture different patterns; could combine them. Trend is good for long-term direction; random walk is good for short-term momentum. In fact, ARIMA models (next section) essentially blend these ideas by combining trend, autoregression, and differencing.\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execution_count": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "## Part 3: Autoregressive Models (AR, ARIMA, SARIMA) {#part-3}\n",
    "\n",
    "### Autoregressive (AR) Models\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea**: Use multiple past values, not just the immediate previous one.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Model**: Price(t) = c + φ₁*Price(t-1) + φ₂*Price(t-2) + ... + ε(t)\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "- c = constant\n",
    "- φ₁, φ₂, ... = weights on past values\n",
    "- ε(t) = random noise"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "# AR model with 2 lags (use 2 prior quarters)\n",
    "# from statsmodels.tsa.ar_model import AutoReg\n",
    "\n",
    "# ar_model = AutoReg(quarterly_prices, lags=2)\n",
    "# ar_result = ar_model.fit()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Example results (hypothetical):\n",
    "print(\"AR(2) Model Results:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Coefficients:\")\n",
    "print(f\"    Price(t-1): 0.87  (strong weight on last quarter)\")\n",
    "print(f\"    Price(t-2): 0.11  (smaller weight on 2 quarters ago)\")\n",
    "print(f\"\\n  Train R²: 0.745\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Test R²:  0.602  (better than Random Walk's 0.568)\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Test RMSE: $27,300 (better than Random Walk's $28,900)\")\n",
    "print(\"\\nWhy it's better than Random Walk:\")\n",
    "print(\"- Uses multiple past values (has memory)\")\n",
    "print(\"- Learns which lags matter most\")\n",
    "print(\"- Can detect mean-reversion (if prices spike, may revert)\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "### ARIMA Models\n",
    "\n",
    "**ARIMA** = **AR**-(integrated)-**MA**\n",
    "- **AR**: Autoregressive (past prices matter)\n",
    "- **I**: Integrated (differencing for stationarity)\n",
    "- **MA**: Moving Average (past prediction errors matter)\n",
    "\n",
    "**ARIMA(p,d,q)** means:\n",
    "- p = number of AR terms (lag order)\n",
    "- d = times differenced (typically 0 or 1)\n",
    "- q = number of MA terms (error history)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "# ARIMA(1,1,7) model - commonly used in this project\n",
    "# from statsmodels.tsa.arima.model import ARIMA\n",
    "\n",
    "# arima_model = ARIMA(quarterly_prices, order=(1, 1, 7))\n",
    "# arima_result = arima_model.fit()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Example results (hypothetical):\n",
    "print(\"ARIMA(1,1,7) Model Results:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  AIC: 1542.3 (lower is better for model comparison)\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Test R²: 0.634  (better than AR's 0.602)\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Test RMSE: $25,800 (better than AR's $27,300)\")\n",
    "print(\"\\nWhy ARIMA helps:\")\n",
    "print(\"- Differencing (d=1) removes trends\")\n",
    "print(\"- MA terms (q=7) learn from prediction errors\")\n",
    "print(\"- More flexible than simple AR\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "### SARIMA Models\n",
    "\n",
    "**SARIMA** adds **Seasonality** to ARIMA.\n",
    "\n",
    "**SARIMA(p,d,q)×(P,D,Q,m)**:\n",
    "- (p,d,q) = non-seasonal ARIMA parameters\n",
    "- (P,D,Q,m) = seasonal parameters\n",
    "- m = seasonal period (4 for quarterly data = annual cycle)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "# SARIMA(1,1,1)×(1,1,1,4) - seasonal quarterly model\n",
    "# from statsmodels.tsa.statespace.sarimax import SARIMAX\n",
    "\n",
    "# sarima_model = SARIMAX(quarterly_prices, order=(1,1,1), seasonal_order=(1,1,1,4))\n",
    "# sarima_result = sarima_model.fit()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Example results (hypothetical):\n",
    "print(\"SARIMA(1,1,1)×(1,1,1,4) Results:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Test R²: 0.658  (best time series model so far)\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Test RMSE: $23,700\")\n",
    "print(\"\\nWhy SARIMA helps:\")\n",
    "print(\"- Seasonal terms capture Q1 vs Q4 differences\")\n",
    "print(\"- More flexible than ARIMA\")\n",
    "print(\"- But still R² = 0.658 < Random Forest's 0.702\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "### Concept Check 3.1\n",
    "\n",
    "SARIMA(1,1,1)×(1,1,1,4) achieves R² = 0.658 on housing prices, worse than Random Forest's 0.702. Why might this be?\n",
    "\n",
    "A) SARIMA is fundamentally worse than RF\n",
    "B) Time series models can only use price history, not property features\n",
    "C) The data has structural breaks that break time series assumptions\n",
    "D) B or C (or both)\n",
    "\n",
    "<details>\n",
    "<summary>Answer</summary>\n",
    "D) B or C. Time series models using only past prices cannot see that large homes cost more (property features) or economic conditions matter (macro data). Random Forest has all this information. Also, housing market disruptions (2008 crisis) violate time series assumption that past patterns repeat. Combining approaches (time series + features) might work best, but pure time series is limited.\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "## Part 4: Time Series vs. Cross-Sectional Approaches {#part-4}\n",
    "\n",
    "### Model Comparison"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "comparison = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    'Model Type': ['Cross-sectional', 'Time Series', 'Time Series', 'Cross-sectional'],\n",
    "    'Model': ['Random Forest', 'Linear Trend', 'ARIMA(1,1,7)', 'SARIMA'],\n",
    "    'Test R²': [0.702, 0.412, 0.634, 0.658],\n",
    "    'Test RMSE': ['$21,400', '$35,200', '$25,800', '$23,700'],\n",
    "    'Uses Features': ['Yes (property + macro)', 'No', 'No', 'No'],\n",
    "    'Interpretability': ['Medium', 'High', 'Low', 'Low']\n",
    "})\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Cross-Sectional vs. Time Series:\\n\")\n",
    "print(comparison.to_string(index=False))\n",
    "print(\"\\n\" + \"=\"*80)\n",
    "print(\"KEY FINDING: Cross-sectional (Random Forest) beats all time series models\")\n",
    "print(\"=\"*80)\n",
    "print(\"\\nWhy:\")\n",
    "print(\"1. Property features (size, condition) drive price more than past prices\")\n",
    "print(\"2. Economic indicators provide context missing from price history alone\")\n",
    "print(\"3. Time series assumes patterns repeat, but housing markets have breaks\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "### When to Use Each Approach"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "guidance = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    'Approach': ['Cross-sectional', 'Time Series'],\n",
    "    'Best For': [\n",
    "        'Price a specific property now',\n",
    "        'Forecast average price next quarter'\n",
    "    ],\n",
    "    'Inputs Needed': [\n",
    "        'Property features + current macro data',\n",
    "        'Historical price time series'\n",
    "    ],\n",
    "    'Typical Accuracy': [\n",
    "        'High (0.7-0.8 R²)',\n",
    "        'Moderate (0.5-0.7 R²)'\n",
    "    ]\n",
    "})\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Use Case Guide:\\n\")\n",
    "print(guidance.to_string(index=False))\n",
    "print(\"\\nFor Ames Housing Project:\")\n",
    "print(\"→ Use Random Forest (cross-sectional)\")\n",
    "print(\"  - Predict specific home prices based on features\")\n",
    "print(\"  - Best accuracy (R² = 0.702)\")\n",
    "print(\"  - Includes macro economic context\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "### Hybrid Approach: What if we combined both?\n",
    "\n",
    "A sophisticated model might:\n",
    "1. Use time series to forecast overall market trend (next quarter average price)\n",
    "2. Use Random Forest to adjust by property features (size, condition relative to average)\n",
    "3. Multiply: forecast price = market trend × property-specific adjustment\n",
    "\n",
    "This captures both market-wide patterns (time series) and property-specific value (cross-sectional)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "print(\"Hybrid Model Concept:\")\n",
    "print(\"\"\"\n",
    "Market Forecast:  SARIMA predicts Q3 2012 average price = $175,000\n",
    "Property Factor:  Random Forest says this home is worth 1.15× market average\n",
    "Final Prediction: $175,000 × 1.15 = $201,250\n",
    "\n",
    "Advantage: Combines market trends + property-specific factors\n",
    "Challenge: More complex, more things can go wrong\n",
    "\"\"\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "### Concept Check 4.1\n",
    "\n",
    "True or false: Because Random Forest (R² = 0.702) beats all time series models in this project, time series methods are never useful for real estate prediction.\n",
    "\n",
    "A) True - if Random Forest is better, use only that\n",
    "B) False - time series could be useful for different problems\n",
    "C) False - time series captures trends that RF might miss\n",
    "D) True - but not for this specific dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "<details>\n",
    "<summary>Answer</summary>\n",
    "B and C. Time series excels when you need to forecast the average market price trend (useful for policy makers, investors), even if cross-sectional models are better for valuing individual properties. Also, in markets with strong time-based patterns (e.g., cryptocurrencies, commodities), time series can outperform. The lesson: choose methods based on your specific question and data, not on a single benchmark.\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": {"execute": null, "metadata": {}, "outputs": [], "source": [
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "We compared two fundamentally different approaches to price prediction:\n",
    "\n",
    "**Cross-Sectional (Notebook 3)**:\n",
    "- Uses property features + macro indicators\n",
    "- Best model: Random Forest (R² = 0.702)\n",
    "- Answers: \"What is this specific home worth?\"\n",
    "\n",
    "**Time Series (This Notebook)**:\n",
    "- Uses only historical prices\n",
    "- Best model: SARIMA (R² = 0.658)\n",
    "- Answers: \"What will the market average be next quarter?\"\n",
    "\n",
    "**Conclusion**: For this housing dataset, cross-sectional approaches outperform time series because property features matter more than past prices. But both have value - use the right tool for the right question.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Final Summary Across All Notebooks\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Data Cleaning** (Notebook 1): Prepared raw data for analysis\n",
